# Answer 1 · The Sites and Forms of Violence against Women in Pakistan, and How to Eliminate It (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-3

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define violence against women, organise its forms by the sites where they occur — home, community, workplace, public spaces, online and the state — then give your own strategy, built on the five Ps, to eliminate it.
@ask ① What are the SITES and FORMS of VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN in Pakistan?
@ask ② How, IN YOUR VIEW, can this MENACE be ELIMINATED from our society?
(1) Introduction: violence occurs at every site of women's lives; ending it needs prevention, protection and justice together
(2) Definition and scale: DEVAW 1993; PDHS 28%; SSDO 32,617 cases (2024)
(3) Site 1 — the home: domestic violence, dowry violence, stove burning, marital rape
(4) Site 1 — the home: honour killing, with HRCP's 405 cases (2024)
(5) Site 2 — the community: jirgas, vani and swara, rape, acid attacks
(6) Site 3 — the workplace: harassment; abuse of domestic workers
(7) Site 4 — public spaces and online: street harassment, cyber violence
(8) Site 5 — the state: custodial abuse, police inaction, discriminatory laws
(9) Why violence persists: patriarchy, dependence, impunity
(10) Elimination 1: prevention — education, men, scholars, media, economic power
(11) Elimination 2: protection, prosecution, services and partnership
(12) Conclusion: from impunity to accountability
:::

## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"Sites and forms"** asks **where** violence happens (home, community, workplace, public spaces, online, the state) and **what kinds** occur at each site — so organise by site, not as a random list. **"How in your view this menace can be eliminated"** asks for your **own strategy**, which must answer the causes you identify. Give data, laws and cases throughout.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
The UN **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women** (==1993==) defines violence against women as any act of gender-based violence that results in **physical, sexual or psychological harm**, including threats, coercion or deprivation of liberty, in **public or private life**.

#### ii) The argument
In Pakistan, violence follows women into **every site** of their lives — the home, the community, the workplace, public spaces, the internet and even the institutions of the state. It persists because of **patriarchal norms, women's economic dependence and impunity**. Eliminating it therefore requires a combined strategy: **prevention** to change norms, **protection** to keep women safe, and **prosecution** to end impunity.

### 2. Definition and scale
The **Pakistan Demographic and Health Survey 2017–18** found that **28%** of ever-married women had experienced physical violence since age 15, and **34%** spousal physical, sexual or emotional violence; **56%** of those who suffered violence never sought help. In ==2024== the **Sustainable Social Development Organization (SSDO)** recorded **32,617** reported cases of gender-based violence. These reported cases are only the visible tip of a much larger problem. Worldwide, the WHO estimates that about **1 in 3** women experience physical or sexual violence in their lifetime, so Pakistan's problem is part of a global pattern — but one made worse by weak enforcement.

### 3. Site 1 — the home: domestic violence

#### i) Domestic violence
The home is the most dangerous site. Women are beaten, burned, starved, confined and threatened by husbands and in-laws; **marital rape** is common but rarely recognised. **Economic abuse** — taking a woman's income or denying her food and money — is widespread.

#### ii) Dowry violence and stove burning
Brides whose families cannot meet **dowry** demands face harassment and beating. Some are burned in "kitchen accidents" — **stove burning** — that are often disguised murders.

### 4. Site 1 — the home: honour killing
Women who choose their own husbands, seek divorce or are merely suspected of a relationship may be killed by fathers, brothers or husbands claiming to defend family **honour** — *karo-kari* in Sindh, *siyahkari* in Balochistan. The **Human Rights Commission of Pakistan** recorded at least **405** honour killings in ==2024==. The murder of **Qandeel Baloch** by her brother in ==2016== showed how family members kill and then claim forgiveness from their own relatives.

### 5. Site 2 — the community

#### i) Parallel justice
**Jirgas** and **panchayats** of male elders order ***vani*** and ***swara*** — giving girls in marriage to settle disputes — and even punishment rape, as in the case of **Mukhtaran Mai** (==2002==).

#### ii) Rape and abduction
In ==2024==, **5,339** rapes and **24,439** kidnappings and abductions were reported. The rape and murder of six-year-old **Zainab Ansari** in Kasur (==2018==) shocked the nation.

#### iii) Acid attacks
Acid is thrown on women who refuse marriage proposals or advances, leaving them blind and disfigured. The **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011** provides 14 years to life imprisonment, but attacks continue.

::: fig grid | The sites of violence against women in Pakistan and the main forms at each.
@cols 3
Home: domestic violence, dowry violence, stove burning, marital rape, honour killing
Community: jirga punishments, vani and swara, rape, abduction, acid attacks
Workplace: sexual harassment; abuse of domestic and home-based workers
Public spaces: street harassment, stalking, assault on transport
Online: cyber-harassment, blackmail, image-based abuse
The state: custodial abuse, police refusal, discriminatory laws
:::

### 6. Site 3 — the workplace
Women in offices, factories, fields and hospitals face **sexual harassment** — unwelcome advances, demands for favours and hostile environments. **Domestic workers**, many of them young girls, are among the most vulnerable, and cases of severe abuse in employers' homes have repeatedly come to light. The **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010**, amended in ==2022==, requires inquiry committees and created ombudspersons, but many workplaces, especially informal ones, do not comply.

### 7. Site 4 — public spaces and online

#### i) Public spaces
**Street harassment**, staring, stalking and groping on buses and in markets restrict women's mobility and keep many from jobs and colleges. **Section 509** of the Penal Code (amended ==2010==) punishes harassment in public places.

#### ii) Online
With smartphones has come **cyber violence** — harassment, fake profiles, blackmail with private images and threats. The **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act 2016** criminalises such acts, but reporting is low.

### 8. Site 5 — the state
The state itself can be a site of violence: **custodial abuse** in police stations, police **refusal** to register complaints, and laws that once discriminated — under the **Zina Ordinance** (==1979==), rape victims who could not prove their case risked prosecution for adultery until the **Protection of Women Act 2006**.

### 9. Why the menace persists

#### i) Patriarchy
Norms of male authority and family **honour** treat women's bodies and choices as men's business.

#### ii) Economic dependence
With female labour-force participation at only ==24.4%==, many women cannot leave abusive homes.

#### iii) Impunity
SSDO found conviction rates of about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing in ==2024==. Police refuse FIRs, families force settlements, and trials take years.

### 10. Elimination 1: prevention

#### i) Education and men
Teach gender equality and respect in schools, keep girls in school, and engage **men and boys** — as the NGO **Rozan** does — to challenge the idea that violence proves manhood.

#### ii) Scholars and media
Mobilise religious scholars to declare honour killing, forced marriage and denial of inheritance contrary to Islam; use dramas and campaigns to show respectful relationships.

#### iii) Economic power
Women's jobs, inheritance and cash transfers through the **Benazir Income Support Programme** increase their bargaining power and their ability to leave abuse.

### 11. Elimination 2: protection, prosecution, services and partnership

#### i) Protection
Extend **helplines** (Punjab's **1043**), **shelters** and one-stop centres like the **Violence against Women Centre in Multan** (==2017==) to every district; enforce protection orders under the **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016**.

#### ii) Prosecution
Make FIR registration compulsory, train police and prosecutors, recruit women officers, build **DNA** and forensic capacity, use **special courts** under the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**, and make honour crimes fully non-compoundable.

#### iii) Services and partnership
Provide free legal aid, medical care and counselling; build a national GBV database; and co-ordinate police, health, courts and NGOs.

::: fig cycle | A strategy to eliminate violence against women — the five Ps.
@centre Ending violence against women
Prevention: education, men and boys, scholars, media
Protection: helplines, shelters, protection orders
Prosecution: FIRs, forensics, special courts, no pardons
Provision: legal aid, health care, counselling, livelihoods
Partnership: state, NGOs, communities, media
:::

### 12. Conclusion
Violence against women in Pakistan is not confined to one place — it reaches women at home, in the community, at work, in the street, online and in custody. Its many forms share common roots in **patriarchy, dependence and impunity**. In my view, the menace can be eliminated only by attacking all three at once: changing norms through education and men's engagement, giving women economic independence, and making justice certain through effective policing and courts. When perpetrators expect punishment rather than pardon, violence will begin to fall.

## Why this answer scores
- Organises forms by **site**, exactly as the question asks.
- Supports every site with **data, laws or cases** (PDHS, SSDO, HRCP, Zainab, Mukhtaran Mai).
- Links causes to a **personal strategy** built on the five Ps.
- A **grid** and a **cycle** figure summarise the analysis and the strategy.
